131
00:13:05,020 --> 00:13:09,520
Production had begun on heavier main battle tanks, Panzer 3s and 4s,

132
00:13:09,521 --> 00:13:13,740
but they were still in short supply during the attacks on Poland and France.

133
00:13:15,720 --> 00:13:19,680
The Panzer III was designed as the primary breakthrough tank.

134
00:13:20,120 --> 00:13:23,020
Unlike most tanks that are more heavily armored up front,

135
00:13:23,460 --> 00:13:26,300
the Mark III was equally armored on all sides.

136
00:13:28,680 --> 00:13:34,620
It was expected that initially these tanks would overrun the enemy's frontline defenses

137
00:13:34,621 --> 00:13:38,680
and might be vulnerable to anti-tank fire from the rear.

138
00:13:39,520 --> 00:13:45,540
The Panzer IV was more heavily armored in front as it was designed to travel with infantry support.

139
00:13:46,300 --> 00:13:52,500
The tank was fitted with a 75-millimeter howitzer to knock out enemy fortifications and machine gun nests.

140
00:13:53,280 --> 00:13:56,400
The Panzer IV became the backbone of the Panzer divisions.

141
00:13:59,700 --> 00:14:08,740
It was not primarily intended as an anti-tank weapon, and indeed during the invasion of France and later Russia,

142
00:14:08,741 --> 00:14:14,520
Early Panzer IVs with the short howitzer were almost completely incapable of knocking out enemy tanks.

143
00:14:16,020 --> 00:14:22,120
The panzers were propelled to victory by decisive leadership and effective command and control.

144
00:14:23,580 --> 00:14:30,280
The armored visionaries like Guderian felt that it was essential to lead armored units from the front

145
00:14:30,281 --> 00:14:36,200
because of the speed of their movement and the fact that they often would encounter unexpected obstacles.

146
00:14:36,201 --> 00:14:43,280
leadership on the spot unhindered by long lines of communication was essential to seize the

147
00:14:43,281 --> 00:14:44,121
initiative.

148
00:14:44,122 --> 00:14:50,700
Panzer tank commanders were trained to think and act independently on the battlefield.

149
00:14:50,701 --> 00:15:00,360
The great difference between us and other armies was that we were not given specific orders we

150
00:15:00,361 --> 00:15:05,580
were given mission orders. If we were given the order to attack a village, I

151
00:15:05,581 --> 00:15:11,820
could decide when and where to do it. It was my decision how I accomplished this

152
00:15:11,821 --> 00:15:18,240
mission. The difference was that I could react to constantly changing conditions

153
00:15:18,241 --> 00:15:25,480
on the battlefield as long as I achieved my goal. A network of sophisticated

154
00:15:25,481 --> 00:15:31,360
communication lines helped coordinate the combined arms attacks.

155
00:15:31,361 --> 00:15:37,280
In a tank division, we had a liaison officer from the Air Force.

156
00:15:37,281 --> 00:15:44,160
And this liaison officer gave the pilots exactly where are the targets.

157
00:15:44,161 --> 00:15:53,080
It was absolutely a revolution in the beginning of the war on our side.

158
00:15:53,081 --> 00:15:57,000
While their opponents communicated with archaic hand signals and flags,

159
00:15:57,520 --> 00:16:00,760
the panzers were fitted with state-of-the-art FM radio gear.

160
00:16:02,120 --> 00:16:07,080
Our tank was equipped with communication lines.

161
00:16:08,360 --> 00:16:11,780
And we can talk from one tank to another.

162
00:16:12,500 --> 00:16:14,940
The platoon commander can talk to all tanks.

163
00:16:14,941 --> 00:16:18,040
And the company commander can do it also.

164
00:16:18,800 --> 00:16:20,620
The leading of the tank was very simple.

165
00:16:20,621 --> 00:16:31,600
We used to have certain codes we applied and which we changed, if possible, from day to day.

166
00:16:32,320 --> 00:16:34,000
I can remember one word.

167
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:38,440
It was enemy infantry was the word Prediger Ölsardinen.

168
00:16:38,580 --> 00:16:41,500
Now that means preacher and sardines.

169
00:16:41,980 --> 00:16:44,600
So, I mean, it is just one thing I remember.

170
00:16:44,601 --> 00:16:50,140
But radio connection was absolutely a must.

171
00:16:50,141 --> 00:16:51,220
and very important.

172
00:16:52,500 --> 00:16:56,280
Over the radio, we can talk all together in the tank.

173
00:16:57,440 --> 00:17:00,740
The crew commander to the driver, the driver to the operator,

174
00:17:01,080 --> 00:17:04,520
the operator to the loader, and to the signal man.

175
00:17:06,140 --> 00:17:07,840
The communication was excellent,

176
00:17:08,280 --> 00:17:12,460
giving by not this microphone that's living here.

177
00:17:13,660 --> 00:17:14,840
It was excellent.

178
00:17:14,841 --> 00:17:21,840
Ingenious throat microphones didn't transmit the deafening tank noise and helped create

179
00:17:21,841 --> 00:17:26,840
an efficiency within the crew unmatched by opposing armies.

180
00:17:26,841 --> 00:17:33,840
In February of 1941, Erwin Rommel, a hero of the Battle of France, was dispatched to

181
00:17:33,841 --> 00:17:38,840
